{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261123T135555Z-05b709ab", "ts": "2026-11-23T14:00:09Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.9.1-dev", "branch": "fix/round-budget-cadence", "build": "95308783037f"}, "body": {"base_commit": "4a381e2d16a2a8c92c026a18e8e1b05edce40145", "base_tree": "004a06b609de8d8c66c09cd31acc0f691d8a8432", "head_tree": "12d7b42a9df55872cf1ca698fa9b11233a42a5b6", "head_commit": "33e76fe15723d1eb2a08a81e45fc153eac20f2d6", "dispatch_commit": "33e76fe15723d1eb2a08a81e45fc153eac20f2d6", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/consumer-build-metrics.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/delegation.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/changes/delegate-ceiling-whole-tree-guards.md", ".prawduct/changes/round-budget-cadence.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "documentation/consumer-build-metrics.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/delegation.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/changes/delegate-ceiling-whole-tree-guards.md", ".prawduct/changes/round-budget-cadence.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "blocking", "title": "The 'owner ruling 2026-11-23' that authorizes the round-budget cadence change is recorded nowhere but in the change itself", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It broke because the ruling was cited in the work but never written down. The change rewrites normative budget policy: review-cycle.md now says the budget 'caps the full rounds one body of work may buy past its plan's cadence', and project-state-keys.md, the template comment, the api-contract and _cadence_round_ids follow it. Every one of those sites gives 'owner ruling 2026-11-23' as its authority: project-state-keys.md:387, the _cadence_round_ids docstring, the TestRoundBudgetCadence docstring, .prawduct/changes/round-budget-cadence.md and the commit message. But .prawduct/owner-rulings.md has no 2026-11-23 entry; its newest entry is 2026-11-22, and .handoff-notes.md also calls that the newest. So every witness to the decision is builder-authored text inside the amendment. norms.md 'What counts as a recorded decision' says 'A builder-authored rationale sentence, alone, is not a recorded decision'. building.md 'When the Owner Rules' says to write the owner's dated words to owner-rulings.md before replying. Core learning: 'A governance change cannot supply its own authority: \u2026 land the owner's confirmation somewhere the amendment isn't'. The ruling's exact scope cannot be checked against the code either. Example: the owner may have meant 'the first boundary review' to include both the `final` and the PR `cumulative` of a standard Medium/Large plan (see the observation below). Remedy: add the 2026-11-23 entry to .prawduct/owner-rulings.md in the owner's words, saying which item answered R-9(a) and where it landed (project-state-keys.md review_round_budget, _cadence_round_ids). Then confirm the implemented rule matches those words. Since owner-rulings.md began (2026-11-10), every other dated owner-ruling citation has a matching entry, so this is a first instance rather than a class.", "files": ["plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Cadence is keyed on review MODE, not on the plan step a review serves: a chunk's declared `Critic mode: final` review takes the scope's one boundary allowance, and the real boundary review is charged", "recommendation": "Why it broke: `_cadence_round_ids` uses mode as a stand-in for 'which plan step this is'. `chunk` gets the per-chunk allowance, and any `final`/`cumulative` gets the single boundary allowance whatever chunks it names. But the methodology lets a mid-plan chunk declare `Critic mode: final`. The shipped template's own Chunk 02 does (plugin/templates/build-plan.md:168), and planning.md:146 lets a chunk that ships as its own PR declare `cumulative`. Those reviews are the plan's steps, not a loop. Today the first one takes the boundary allowance, so the plan's actual boundary review (and every later chunk-level `final`) is charged. A plan with k final-declared chunks is charged k of its cadence rounds, so a plan like build-plan-learnings-v2-delete (5 final-declared chunks) is back at the refusal this change set out to end. A read of this clone's evidence store finds 31 `final` reviews that name chunks. In 13 of 204 scopes, a chunk-naming `final` took the first-boundary slot ahead of another boundary review. Scope: class, closed by one owner. In `_cadence_round_ids`, key the per-chunk allowance on 'a full review naming a chunk no earlier full review named', whatever its mode. Give the boundary allowance to the first whole-plan review: one that names no chunk, or one that names the plan's last chunk. Then add a test with a mid-plan chunk-naming `final` followed by a `cumulative`, red before the change. `test_a_boundary_review_naming_chunks_does_not_spend_a_chunks_allowance` pins the current separation, so revisit it with the change. If the owner ruling deliberately limited the allowance to `chunk` mode, record that edge in project-state-keys.md and the change record instead, so the gap is ruled rather than incidental.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The owner ruling of 2026-11-23 that authorizes the cadence allowance is cited five times on this branch but recorded on none of it", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the ruling was written to .prawduct/owner-rulings.md on fix/chunk-plan-order (commit 32a0ff59, item 2), and this fix was cut off develop as that ruling directs, so it carries the citations without the entry. At 33e76fe1 the docstring of critic_consolidate._cadence_round_ids, plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md (review_round_budget), the TestRoundBudgetCadence docstring, the change record .prawduct/changes/round-budget-cadence.md and the commit message all cite 'owner ruling 2026-11-23'. This tree's owner-rulings.md has no 2026-11-23 entry, and neither does origin/develop, the PR target, which does not contain 32a0ff59. If this PR lands on develop before fix/chunk-plan-order, develop gets a gate-behaviour change whose only witness is the change itself. A later session or Critic checking that authority in owner-rulings.md (building.md 'When the Owner Rules': 'later sessions and people read that file') finds nothing, and the change reads as an agent amending its own budget. Core learning: 'A governance change cannot supply its own authority: ... land the owner's confirmation somewhere the amendment isn't'. Fix: carry the 2026-11-23 entry verbatim on this branch. Identical text should merge cleanly or trivially with chunk-plan-order's copy. Alternatively, make this PR's merge wait explicitly on fix/chunk-plan-order and say so in the PR. Pick one; a later merge order nobody stated is not enough. The implementation matches the ruling as written: each chunk's first chunk review and the scope's first final or cumulative review are free, and 6 stays the cap on everything after.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-3"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 2, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "In the documented Medium/Large cadence, the allowance covers only one of the plan's two boundary reviews, so the PR's required cumulative is charged", "recommendation": "review-cycle.md's table gives a chunked plan without `Type: cumulative-final` this cadence: `chunk` per non-final chunk, then `final` on the last chunk. `/prawduct:pr create` then requires a `cumulative`. _cadence_round_ids frees only the scope's first boundary review, so in that shape the planned PR cumulative spends one budget round. A 7-chunk plan reaches it at spent=0 and leaves it at spent=1, so nothing is refused at the default of 6. A repo with budget 1 would be refused its next round, though. The behavior is pinned as intended (test_only_the_first_boundary_review_is_cadence[cumulative]), so the only question is whether it matches the owner's words. Settle that when the ruling is recorded (finding above); no code change is implied otherwise.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation had nothing to reconcile", "recommendation": "No live plan claims fix/round-budget-cadence (scope unbound), and the change fragment carries no governed_by: dispositions, so there was no recorded `conforms`/`inapplicable` to test against the diff. The authority question this reconciliation would have asked is the BLOCKING finding above. No action beyond that.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/round-budget-cadence.md"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "`_cadence_round_ids` reaches two more private names across modules (`plan_status._WHOLE_PLAN_MODES`, `buildplan_refs._normalize_chunk_id`)", "recommendation": "There is precedent: this module already calls `buildplan_refs._normalize_chunk_id` at five other sites and `plan_status._WHOLE_PLAN_MODES` at one, and the normalizer's docstring calls itself the tree's only normalizer. Both are de facto public. No edit is wanted for this change. If a later change touches these, promote them to public names in one move rather than one call site at a time.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "review-cycle.md now says the budget caps rounds 'past its plan's cadence' without saying what cadence is", "recommendation": "The definition (each chunk's first `chunk` review plus the first boundary review) lives in project-state-keys.md, which review-cycle does not cite here. A builder reading only review-cycle cannot tell which of their reviews are free. This is prose that nothing reads, so it is an observation. If the line is ever touched again, make it relational, for example by pointing at `docs/project-state-keys.md` 'review_round_budget'.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Unchanged describers of the budget now omit the cadence carve-out", "recommendation": "Two descriptions outside the diff still describe the old count. The `REVIEW_ROUND_BUDGET_DEFAULT` comment in plugin/lib/core.py:791 still reads 'Full review rounds one body of work may buy'. documentation/consumer-build-metrics.md:128 says repeat cumulatives sit outside the budget's reach 'until the sixth round', a reach that now starts after the free first boundary review. Neither is read by a gate or a test, and both stay approximately true, so no edit is wanted. The core.py comment's calibration ('six sits above every measured chain') was measured with cadence rounds counted. Under the new count the effective ceiling is higher, which is a note for whoever next re-derives the default.", "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The refusal reason prints 'not counting the 0 its plan's cadence bought' when no cadence round exists", "recommendation": "This is harmless wording on the unscoped-chunk and loop-only shapes. Dropping the clause when the count is 0 would read better, but no edit is wanted.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Records Pass: covered all four oracle files; nothing else clears a bar", "recommendation": "Covered: .prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md, .prawduct/changes/round-budget-cadence.md, .prawduct/changes/delegate-ceiling-whole-tree-guards.md, .prawduct/owner-rulings.md. Each claim checks out against the code and tests. The api-contract exit-4 line matches _cadence_round_ids. In round-budget-cadence.md, the cadence=N rendering, the absence-means-predates key, the counted no-chunk chunk review and its consumer note all match. In delegate-ceiling-whole-tree-guards.md, the three pinned sentences and the narrow-green tell are real and tested. The new 2026-11-22 owner-rulings entry matches where item 3 landed (delegation.md). The one record gap, the change record citing a ruling absent from this tree, is part of the finding above. No edit wanted beyond that.", "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Goal 6: the cadence allowance is observable at the refusal and in its record", "recommendation": "No action. The refusal reason names the cadence count. The guard-refusal fact carries 'cadence', and evidence list renders it as cadence=N. A record missing the key renders nothing rather than zero, so old records are not misread. The only reader of guard-refusal 'spent' (evidence._cmd_list) shows cadence beside it, so the change in what 'spent' means reaches its one consumer. A within-budget dispatch still says nothing about rounds