w\". The ask the owner approved (this session's transcript, the turn answered by \"Yes to all of your recommendations\") reads \"not counting the first review of each chunk or the first branch-wide review\". The substance the note relies on holds: the ask named no mode. But a passage in quotation marks in the rulings file should match the source. If the entry is touched again, copy the words exactly.", "about": {"review_id": "rev-20261123T135555Z-05b709ab", "fid": "R-1"}, "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Three describers still state the old chunk-mode-only cadence rule", "recommendation": "Class: every sentence describing which reviews the cadence frees. The fix changed the rule in _cadence_round_ids, project-state-keys.md, api-contract.md and the change record. Three describers still say \"each chunk's first `chunk` review\": the begin_review comment at plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py:4569, documentation/consumer-build-metrics.md:90 (the predicate-bounds paragraph), and the TestRoundBudgetCadence class docstring at tests/test_critic_consolidate.py:10048. No gate or test reads any of them, so this is an observation. Search for \"first `chunk` review\" and make each sentence relational (point at _cadence_round_ids or project-state-keys.md) when an already-planned commit touches it.", "about": {"review_id": "rev-20261123T135555Z-05b709ab", "fid": "R-2"}, "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "A whole-plan review that names a new chunk leaves the boundary allowance to the next whole-plan review, which may be a re-review", "recommendation": "In _cadence_round_ids, a `final`/`cumulative` naming a new chunk takes that chunk's allowance and does not set boundary_seen. Take a `Type: cumulative-final` plan whose last chunk is reviewed in `cumulative` naming 07. The next `cumulative`, even a re-review of the same work, is then the free boundary review. The cost is at most one uncharged loop round per scope, and it fits the owner's \"first branch-wide review\" wording either way, so no change is implied. It is noted so the boundary review can rule on it knowingly.", "about": {"review_id": "rev-20261123T135555Z-05b709ab", "fid": "R-2"}, "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "The carried ruling entry is no longer byte-identical to fix/chunk-plan-order's copy", "recommendation": "Compared with origin/fix/chunk-plan-order:.prawduct/owner-rulings.md, this branch's 2026-11-23 block is identical except for the five-line transcription note under item 2. Both branches append that block at the same spot off develop, so whichever merges second will conflict there. The resolution is trivial: keep this branch's version, since it is the superset. R-3's 'merge cleanly' premise now holds only as 'merge trivially'. Say so in the PR body so the merger isn't surprised.", "about": {"review_id": "rev-20261123T135555Z-05b709ab", "fid": "R-3"}, "oid": "O-4"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/round-budget-cadence`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "base_reviewed": null, "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/changes/round-budget-cadence.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "documentation/consumer-build-metrics.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0, "late-requirement-record": 0}}, "span": null, "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": null, "dispatched_at": "2026-11-23T14:08:11Z"}}
